title ML-DSA identity front door GREEN — the post-quantum successor to Kumara's Ed25519 identity
prompt Continue the loop Lindy-first crux-first past the closed post-quantum ladder; approve my own design rounds and make progress before checking in; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates; a breach rename records a cairn first.
think The whole post-quantum arc stands whole — both ML-KEM (ring·encode·sample·keygen·encaps·decaps) and ML-DSA (ring·encode·sample·keygen·sign·verify) are GREEN and sent, twelve rungs. The pivot doc (20260816-161537) and the last session log's recommend both name the next durable crux as the COMPOSITION: the front door that gives the primitives their reason for being. Between a PQ sealed session over ML-KEM and a PQ Kumara identity over ML-DSA, the identity is the more Lindy — the signature every Kumara name, subkey, attestation, and Lotus carry ultimately rests on. So the crux is mldsa_identity.rye, the ML-DSA sibling of crypto/kumara_identity.rye.
think Read crypto/kumara_identity.rye end to end for the composition pattern (a FRONT DOOR that authors no new cryptography, only wiring plus a multi-way proof), then the exact keygen/sign/verify signatures. keygen(comptime k,l,eta,...), sign and verify take all eight comptime params (k,l,eta,gamma1_bits,gamma2,tau,omega,ctilde). Lattice keys do not derive down an HD tree the way SLIP-0010 ed25519 does, so a PQ identity is simpler than the classical one: a 32-byte seed ξ → (pk, sk) via ML-DSA.KeyGen, and sign/verify wrappers — no path walk.
think Bundled the eight FIPS 204 constants per set into a Params value (ml_dsa_44/65/87), named ml_dsa_65 the default (NIST category 3, the balanced durable choice for a decades-lasting identity). IdentityType(comptime P) returns a struct sized exactly per set from pk_len/sk_len/sig_len computed off FIPS 204's own formulas; from_seed/sign/verify are comptime-parameterized free functions. Avoided the banned Self = @This() by returning the type from a function rather than a namespace with a Self alias.
think The composition's proof mirrors the classical door's spirit, four ways over every set and four seeds with two messages each: (1) NO DRIFT — from_seed's keys equal a hand call to keygen over the same seed, byte-for-byte; (2) DETERMINISM — same seed → identical identity, changed seed → different key; (3) A REAL WORKING SIGNING KEY — sign→verify accepts, while a flipped message byte, a flipped signature byte, and a different identity's public key each refuse; (4) THE DOUBLED ORACLE — the identity's public key equals Zig std.crypto newKeyFromSeed's pk byte-for-byte, and Zig's own Signature.verify accepts the signature this front door produced. The rungs beneath already prove byte-parity; this proves the WIRING and ties it back to the stranger end to end.
think One build fix: Signature is a member of the top-level Dsa type (std.crypto.sign.mldsa.MLDSA65), not of the SecretKey — @TypeOf(kp.sk).Signature had no such member. Changed to Dsa.Signature and it built and greened on the first real run at all three sets.
obs GREEN — crypto/mldsa_identity.rye, the post-quantum identity front door, agrees with Zig std.crypto on the public key and the signature verdict at ML-DSA-44/65/87 over 4 deterministic seeds and 2 messages each. Count guard moved 72→73 files (fifty primitives + twenty-three compositions); the bijection scan is intact (the witness is registered, the file exists). TAME style bans clean, width-check exit 0, living-docs lint advisory-only GREEN.
obs Docs synced in the same round: crypto/README.md and docs/CRYPTO.md each carry the mldsa_identity row and the 72→73 / 22→23 spelled counts; tools/crypto_suite_witness.rish registers the witness and updated all four of its spelled counts. No count claim was left stale — the count guard is the authority and it reads 73.
loom witness=crypto_mldsa_identity_witness param_sets=3 seeds_each=4 msgs_each=2 count_guard_files=73
